            {
                "entry_id": "fc97e99e-6231-3bae-9943-35a17cde70c1",
                "word": "virgil",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "d3a60e37-a4a2-4865-8fa1-d623e2a2c990",
                        "entry_id": "fc97e99e-6231-3bae-9943-35a17cde70c1",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "virgils"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "de8fd9cd-ef95-30df-b041-846779b0b557",
                        "entry_id": "fc97e99e-6231-3bae-9943-35a17cde70c1",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Synonym of slash ⟨/⟩."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "UK",
                            "archaic"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1b5c8376-5db6-48e7-a8d2-7067cb72c7f9",
                                "sense_id": "de8fd9cd-ef95-30df-b041-846779b0b557",
                                "text": "There be five manner of points and divisions most used among cunning men; the which if they be well used, make the sentence very light and easy to be understood, both to the reader and hearer: and they be these, virgil,—come,—parenthesis,—plain point,—interrogative... it is a slender stroke leaning forward, betokening a little short rest, without any perfectness yet of sentence."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3b66e02a-c33d-4201-9689-419778f1d463",
                                "sense_id": "de8fd9cd-ef95-30df-b041-846779b0b557",
                                "text": "No points were used by the ancient printers, excepting the colon and the period; but, after some time, a short oblique stroke, called a virgil, was introduced, which answered to the modern comma."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ee996591-66c0-4dd0-a592-2a90ce304d3b",
                                "sense_id": "de8fd9cd-ef95-30df-b041-846779b0b557",
                                "text": "Whoever introduced the several points, it seems that a full-point, a point called come, answering to our colon-point, a point called virgil answering to our comma-point, the parenthesis-points and interrogative-point, were used at the close of the fourteenth, or beginning of the fifteenth century."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7ce35e94-29ec-44f3-8cf9-2c93b3368557",
                                "sense_id": "de8fd9cd-ef95-30df-b041-846779b0b557",
                                "text": "Other Chaucerian manuscripts had the virgule (or virgil or oblique: /) at the middle of lines."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": [
